                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Cleft sentences",
                    "explanation": "Las cleft sentences dividen una frase en dos para poner el foco en la información importante o en un contraste:\n• What-cleft: What + oración + be + foco. What scares me most is the money.\n• It-cleft: It + be + foco + who/that + resto. It was you who saved the project (no fue Helen).\n• All-cleft: All + oración + be + foco = lo único. All I'm saying is, don't let fear decide.\n• Otras: The reason (why)… is that… / Where… is… / The thing is…\n\nSon muy comunes en conversación y en presentaciones: ordenan el discurso y destacan lo nuevo.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Tipo",
                        "Estructura",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "What-cleft",
                          "What + sujeto + verbo + be + foco",
                          "What scares me most is the money."
                        ],
                        [
                          "It-cleft",
                          "It + be + foco + who/that…",
                          "It was you who saved the museum project."
                        ],
                        [
                          "All-cleft",
                          "All + sujeto + verbo + be…",
                          "All I'm saying is, don't let fear decide."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The reason…",
                          "The reason (why)… is that…",
                          "The reason I'm not worried is that you always have a plan."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Lugar / persona",
                          "Where… is / It's… that",
                          "Where I'd need help is the technical side."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The thing is…",
                          "introducir el problema",
                          "The thing is, they already ask for you."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "What I keep asking myself is whether I'm ready.",
                      "It's not Mark I'm worried about.",
                      "It was you who saved the museum project, not Helen.",
                      "What I'm offering is a partner."
                    ]
                  }
                },
